<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bantu Education Act, 1953</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bantu Education Act of 1953 was a South African apartheid law that placed African schooling under the central government's Native Affairs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> and enforced a separate, deliberately inferior curriculum for black children. Most mission schools, which had educated many African leaders, were closed or taken over. Opposition to it, and later to the imposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> as a teaching language, fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before 1948 African schooling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> rested mainly on the churches. By 1910 church missions paid 90% of the cost of running an African school, and in 1926 they ran 2,702 schools with 215,956 pupils, against 68 state schools with 7,710. Government spending on Black education was fixed at R680,000 a year from 1922 until 1945, while spending on white education grew by 263% between 1910 and 1948. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> came to power in 1948, 97% of schools for Black children were run by churches.</p><p>In 1949 the government set up the Commission on Bantu Education, known as the Eiselen Commission after its chairman, W. W. M. Eiselen, a professor of social anthropology at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>. Its members were all white, and its task was to devise an education for Africans that fitted the aims of apartheid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>, the Minister of Native Affairs, said: &quot;There is no place for [the Bantu] in the European community above the level of certain forms of labour.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The act, No. 47 of 1953, placed Black schools and teacher training colleges under the direct control of the state, which could appoint and train teachers as it saw fit and oversaw school boards and parents' committees. Funding from general revenue was fixed at R13 million from 1955 until 1972, and anything more came from a share of the taxes paid by Black South Africans. Instruction was in the pupils' mother tongue, by the 1960s throughout primary school, with English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> taught as subjects.</p><p>Schools could not hold the new pupils, so the day was split into two sessions of three hours; by 1958, 70 per cent of primary schools taught in double sessions. There was one teacher for 45.3 pupils in 1955 and one for 54.7 in 1960. In 1975–76 the state spent R644 on each white pupil and R42 on each Black pupil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Teachers' associations opposed the new school boards, though not the ANC's school boycott, and the ANC's Freedom Charter of 1955 called for education that was free, compulsory and the same for all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a> said that one of its deep-seated intentions was &quot;to erase all African leadership.&quot; The Extension of University Education Act of 1959 carried the system to the universities. The act was amended in 1956, 1958, 1959 and 1961, and was repealed in 1979 by an act that kept segregated schooling but ended the separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Bantu Education.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bantu Education Act, 1953” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bantu Education</b> gave African children deliberately inferior schooling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act of 1949 and the Immorality Act banned marriage and sex across the colour line; the Suppression of Communism Act of 1950 gave the state wide powers against its opponents; and the <b>Bantu Education Act, 1953</b>, imposed an inferior schooling on African children.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
